Stateless vs Stateful
Stateless	Stateful
Delete the pod, data is lost (stored on the pod/node)	Delete the pod, data survives (stored in external storage such as AWS EBS)

Persistent Volumes exist independently of pods, so they suit databases and other stateful apps.

PV and PVC
Pod
 ↓
PVC (request for storage)
 ↓
PV (actual storage)
 ↓
Physical disk / cloud storage (EBS, EFS)
Component	Purpose
PV	Actual storage, created by an admin or dynamically by a StorageClass
PVC	A user's request for storage (size, access mode, class)
Pod	Mounts the PVC, never the PV directly
Kubernetes matches a PVC to a PV by size, access mode and storage class; the PVC then shows Bound.
If no PV matches, a new one is provisioned dynamically (if a StorageClass supports it) or the PVC stays Pending.
One PVC binds to exactly one PV.
If volumes live inside the cluster, deleting the cluster deletes them. Use external storage like AWS EBS.
Access Modes
Mode	Nodes	Pods	Access	Typical use
RWO (ReadWriteOnce)	Single node	Multiple (same node)	Read/Write	Databases, EBS
ROX (ReadOnlyMany)	Multiple	Multiple	Read-only	Shared static data
RWX (ReadWriteMany)	Multiple	Multiple	Read/Write	Shared storage, EFS/NFS
RWOP (ReadWriteOncePod)	Single pod	One pod	Read/Write	Strict single-pod workloads
Reclaim Policy
Policy	Meaning
Retain	PV and data kept after PVC deletion (status Released, admin cleans up manually)
Delete	PV and underlying storage deleted automatically (good for test/temp)
Recycle	Deprecated
Static Provisioning (EBS)
Create a 20GB EBS volume in AWS.
Create the PV:
yaml
# pv.yml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: my-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 20Gi
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  awsElasticBlockStore:
    volumeID: vol-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
    fsType: ext4
Create the PVC:
yaml
# pvc.yml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: my-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 20Gi
Use it in a Deployment:
yaml
# deploy.yml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: pvdeploy
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: bank
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: bank
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: cont1
        image: nginx
        command: ["/bin/bash", "-c", "sleep 10000"]
        volumeMounts:
        - name: my-bankpvc
          mountPath: "/tmp/persistent"
      volumes:
      - name: my-bankpvc
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: my-pvc
bash
kubectl create -f pv.yml
kubectl create -f pvc.yml
kubectl create -f deploy.yml
kubectl get pv,pvc,pods
Prove persistence
bash
kubectl exec -it <pod> -- /bin/bash
cd /tmp/persistent && touch file{1..5}
exit

kubectl delete pod <pod>          # a new pod is created automatically
kubectl get pods
kubectl exec -it <new-pod> -- ls /tmp/persistent   # files are still there

Note: a PVC can stay Pending until a pod uses it (WaitForFirstConsumer). Check kubectl get events.

Resizing

Increase the EBS volume, then update the PVC size. Volumes can only grow, never shrink. In modern clusters the StorageClass must have allowVolumeExpansion: true.

Dynamic Provisioning (StorageClass)

A StorageClass creates PVs automatically when a PVC asks for one. No manual PV or EBS creation needed (enabled by default in kOps).

bash
kubectl get storageclass
yaml
# ebs-pvc.yml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: ebs-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi
  storageClassName: gp2   # or gp3
yaml
# ebs-pod.yml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: ebs-nginx
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: nginx
    volumeMounts:
    - mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
      name: ebs-volume
  volumes:
  - name: ebs-volume
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: ebs-pvc
bash
kubectl apply -f ebs-pvc.yml
kubectl apply -f ebs-pod.yml
kubectl get pvc,pv,pod

The PVC shows Bound, and the EBS volume appears as in-use in the AWS console.

Internal flow:

PVC created → StorageClass detected → CSI driver invoked → cloud disk created → PV auto-created → PVC bound

Cleanup

bash
kubectl delete pvc my-pvc -n default
kubectl delete pv my-pv
StatefulSet

A StatefulSet manages stateful apps: stable pod names, stable DNS, ordered deployment, and one PVC per pod.

yaml
# nginxstateful.yml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: web
spec:
  serviceName: "nginx"
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
          name: web
        volumeMounts:
        - name: www
          mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
  volumeClaimTemplates:
  - metadata:
      name: www
    spec:
      accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 1Gi
bash
kubectl create -f nginxstateful.yml
kubectl get pv,pvc,pods
kubectl delete pvc www-web-0 www-web-1 www-web-2   # cleanup

Stateful is a characteristic of an application; StatefulSet is the Kubernetes resource used to run it.

Multi-Container Pod

Usually 1 pod = 1 container. Use multiple containers when they must closely collaborate (main app + logging/monitoring sidecar).

yaml
# multicont.yml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: multi-container-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx-container
    image: nginx:latest
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
  - name: logger-container
    image: busybox:latest
    command: ["sh", "-c", "while true; do echo Hello from the logger container; sleep 5; done"]
bash
kubectl create -f multicont.yml
kubectl logs multi-container-pod -c logger-container
kubectl logs multi-container-pod -c nginx-container
Interview Questions

PV vs PVC? PV is the actual storage; PVC is the request for storage.

Why PV/PVC? Containers are ephemeral. PV/PVC give persistent, durable storage decoupled from the pod lifecycle.

Why PVC instead of using a PV directly? It decouples the application from the storage implementation.

What happens when a PVC is deleted? Depends on the reclaim policy: Retain keeps PV and data; Delete removes the backend disk.

Can multiple pods use the same PVC? Yes, if the storage supports RWX (NFS, EFS). Not with most block storage like EBS.

Can one PVC bind to multiple PVs? No, only one.

Why is a PVC stuck in Pending? No matching PV, wrong access mode, insufficient size, or missing StorageClass.

Static vs dynamic provisioning?

Static	Dynamic
Admin creates PV manually	Kubernetes creates PV automatically
Manual work	Automated, modern approach

What is CSI? Container Storage Interface, which lets external storage providers (e.g., AWS EBS CSI) plug into Kubernetes.

hostPath vs PV? hostPath is node-local and not production-ready; PV is a proper, portable persistence abstraction.

StatefulSet and PVC? A StatefulSet creates a separate PVC for each pod (MySQL, MongoDB, etc.).

Can you resize/shrink a PVC? Increase yes, decrease no.

What if a node crashes? With network/cloud storage the pod reschedules, the volume reattaches, and data stays safe.

emptyDir vs PVC? emptyDir is temporary and deleted with the pod; PVC survives pod deletion.

Volume binding modes? Immediate binds right away; WaitForFirstConsumer waits until the pod is scheduled (useful in multi-zone clusters).

volumes vs volumeMounts? volumes defines the storage source; volumeMounts mounts it at a path inside the container.

Block vs file storage?

Block (EBS, Azure Disk)	File (EFS, NFS)
Usually RWO, faster	Usually RWX, shared access

Troubleshooting commands

bash
kubectl get pv
kubectl get pvc
kubectl describe pv
kubectl describe pvc
kubectl get storageclass
kubectl get events

Check events, CSI logs, node attach issues, and access mode mismatches.

Version note: the in-tree awsElasticBlockStore volume type is removed in recent Kubernetes versions. On newer clusters use the AWS EBS CSI driver (csi: volume source or a CSI-backed StorageClass).
